  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-ngc6334&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:09+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SCO" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-35.95920683659464" Magnitude="0" Name="NGC 6334, The Cat’s Paw Nebula" Opacity="100" RA="17.338182716609538" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc6334/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.0427247999999985">
    <Description>This image of emission nebula NGC6334 (the Cat’s Paw Nebula), a star-forming region in the constellation Scorpius, was taken in 2007 using the Mosaic-2 imager on the Blanco 4-meter telescope at Cerro Tololo Inter-American Observatory. The colors of the nebula are reddened by intervening dust in the plane of the Milky Way galaxy. The image was taken as part of a continuing campaign of public-release images using both NOAO 4-meter telescopes.</Description>
