  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="6.389279999999998" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-wlm-dwarf&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:01.741303+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CET" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-15.4175937627213" Magnitude="0" Name="The WLM Local Group dwarf galaxy" Opacity="100" RA="0.0352145619587176" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wlm-dwarf/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.389279999999998">
    <Description>The WLM Local Group dwarf galaxy, as observed with the CTIO Blanco 4-m telescope and Mosaic II camera as part of the NOAO-sponsored Local Group Survey (PI: Philip Massey, Lowell Observatory). The image is a true-color combination of images in three of the filters used by the survey. The full complement of survey data include images in broad-band/ UBVRI/, as well as through filters centered on the H-alpha, [SII], and [OIII] emission lines. All of the data, description of the analysis, and UBVRI photometry are available through the Local Group Survey website.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.2287999999999997" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="0.5830277478472217" CenterY="-15.524220142205854" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The WLM Local Group dwarf galaxy" OffsetX="0.05837822339999998" OffsetY="0.10535490877499998" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.06003790000003934" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wlm-dwarf/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-wlm-dwarf" Xcxstatus="in:642762e2975014bc743f1747">
        <Credits>P. Massey/Lowell Observatory and K. Olsen/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-wlm-dwarf/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wlm-dwarf/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02176&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:07+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="ORI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-5.414609790957386" Magnitude="0" Name="The great nebula in Orion, M42" Opacity="100" RA="5.58559878486033" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02176/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.438239999999999">
    <Description>This picture shows the great nebula in the constellation of Orion the Hunter. On a good clear night, from a dark site well away from the lights of modern civilization, this glowing cloud of gas and dust can be seen with the naked eye as a fuzzy patch surrounding the star Theta Orionis in the Hunter&amp;#x27;s Sword, below Orion&amp;#x27;s belt. It is probably the most spectacular of all the objects cataloged by Charles Messier and now called by their `M&amp;#x27; numbers. M42 had been known since the beginnings of recorded astronomy as a star, but it is so outstanding that it was first noted as an extended nebula in 1610, only a year after Galileo&amp;#x27;s first use of the telescope. Detailed descriptions started appearing later in the seventeenth century, and it has been a popular target for anyone with a telescope ever since. So many details are visible in even a small telescope that M42 will more than repay the observer who makes it a frequent target, and who will find that it is hard to make a realistic sketch that can capture all of the finer features. M42 is our closest example of an HII region, being composed mainly of ionized hydrogen which gives off the red glow so dominant in every picture of the nebula. Deep photographs such as this one show that it is nearly a degree across, larger than the full Moon (although the Moon is so bright that it looks much larger). The energy to keep the nebula glowing comes from the very hot young stars in a formation called the Trapezium, embedded in the brightest part of the nebula and not visible in this photograph. The nebula and the brighter stars are very young indeed by astronomical standards, at about 30000 years. Compare this to our own Sun, which is considered to be a middle-aged star at over 4 billion years! M42 probably contains several hundred stars younger than a million years, still bursting with the energy of youth. Stars are still being born in a dense cloud behind the nebula, but they are hidden from our view by a concentration of dust which reduces their light to only a million-millionth of its original intensity. Fortunately, astronomers have developed special cameras and other detectors which are sensitive to infra-red radiation, more popularly known as heat, which penetrates the dust and reveals to us this stellar nursery. Although M42 is mostly hydrogen, in both neutral and ionized states, with a fair quantity of dust, it does contain significant amounts of other elements, especially oxygen. The green glow of doubly-ionized oxygen is strongest near the intense ultraviolet starlight at the middle of the nebula. To the north-east (the lower left in this picture, which is oriented with north to the left) is a feature called the Dark Bay, which is a thick cloud of neutral gas which has not yet been ionized. M42, NGC 1976 Location: 05h 35.4m -05deg27m (2000). Distance: nearly 500 parsecs (1600 light-years). Size: 66x60 arc minutes Mass: about 300 solar masses Magnitude: 4.0. Power source: O and B stars. Photograph: Bill Schoening, KPNO 4m telescope, 1973.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.0772479999999998" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="83.7770134" CenterY="-5.280848625" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The great nebula in Orion, M42" OffsetX="0.13373279619029996" OffsetY="-0.007469304673999997" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.77382788147077" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02176/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-02176" Xcxstatus="in:64276267975014bc743f1643">
        <Credits>Bill Schoening/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-02176/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02176/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="6.4474592280306435" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noirlab2101b&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2021-01-05T12:36:42+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="PER" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="40.86506760026164" Magnitude="0" Name="NGC 1003 (wide crop)" Opacity="100" RA="2.6556373012212084" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2101b/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.4474592280306435">
    <Description>A stunning long-exposure observation lasting 70 minutes from the Nicholas U. Mayall 4-meter Telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory, a Program of NSF’s NOIRLab, reveals the surroundings of the spiral galaxy NGC 1003 in glorious detail. NGC 1003 resides in front of a galaxy cluster — a vast collection of galaxies bound together by gravity. The long exposure time of this deep observation allowed these usually overlooked red background cluster galaxies to be captured.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.1921709924007935" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="39.83465563456212" CenterY="40.86460417911158" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="NGC 1003 (wide crop)" OffsetX="7.27643427979e-05" OffsetY="7.27643427979e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.06" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2101b/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noirlab2101b" Xcxstatus="in:6427630f975014bc743f17a8">
        <Credits>KPNO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2101b/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2101b/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;eso0848a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2008-12-16T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="MON" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="9.60278156353886" Magnitude="0" Name="NGC 2264 and the Christmas Tree cluster*" Opacity="100" RA="6.682876032266292" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso0848a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.479436333854856">
    <Description>This colour image of the region known as NGC 2264 — an area of sky that includes the sparkling blue baubles of the Christmas Tree star cluster and the Cone Nebula — was created from data taken through four different filters (B, V, R and H-alpha) with the Wide Field Imager at ESO&amp;#x27;s La Silla Observatory, 2400 m high in the Atacama Desert of Chile in the foothills of the Andes. The image shows a region of space about 30 light-years across. This image is available as a mounted image in the ESOshop. #L</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.0830128429727812" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="100.221416089" CenterY="9.6046709646" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="NGC 2264 and the Christmas Tree cluster*" OffsetX="-0.02146370196920399" OffsetY="-0.001300830422376015" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="1.5702596170160756" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso0848a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|eso0848a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>ESO</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0848a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso0848a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;potw1831a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2018-07-30T06:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-57.9247358067404" Magnitude="0" Name="Stormy seas in Carina" Opacity="100" RA="10.3017810249452" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1831a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.485442692689156">
    <Description>This ESO Picture of the Week shows a crescent-shaped cocoon of gas and dust  — a nebula known as NGC 3199, which lies 12 000 light-years away from Earth. It appears to plough through the star-studded sky like a ship through stormy seas. This imagery is very appropriate due to NGC 3199’s location in Carina — a  southern constellation which is named after the keel of a ship! NGC 3199 was discovered by British astronomer John Herschel in 1834 as he compiled his famous catalogue of interesting night sky objects. The nebula has been the subject of numerous observations since, including those by ESO’s 8.2-metre Very Large Telescope (eso0310, eso1117), and 2.6-metre VLT Survey Telescope (VST). The latter made the observations that comprise this image. The nebula’s bright crescent feature is now known to be part of a much larger but fainter bubble of gas and dust. The nebula contains a notable star named HD 89358, which is an unusual type of extremely hot and massive star known as a Wolf-Rayet star. HD 89358 generates incredibly intense stellar winds and outflows that smash into and sweep up the surrounding material, contributing to NGC 3199’s twisted and lopsided morphology. The VST, which began operations in 2011, can image a large area of sky at once — an area twice the size of the full Moon — with its 256-megapixel camera, OmegaCAM. This allows it to characterise interesting objects which its larger neighbour, ESO’s Very Large Telescope, can then explore in even greater detail.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.9734979237511167" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="154.526603679" CenterY="-57.924795328" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Stormy seas in Carina" OffsetX="5.941759788519999e-05" OffsetY="5.941759788519999e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="89.89999999999996" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1831a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|potw1831a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>ESO</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1831a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1831a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02175&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:03+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-59.69753903794027" Magnitude="0" Name="The Eta Carinae nebula, NGC 3372" Opacity="100" RA="10.746542060124675" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02175/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.4872">
    <Description>The central portion of the Eta Carinae Nebula, NGC 3372, also known as the Keyhole Nebula, in the constellation of Carina. This gaseous bright nebula surrounds the peculiar variable star Eta Carinae, with overlying clouds of dark material. The nebula is 9000 light-years from Earth. Cerro Tololo 4-meter Blanco telescope, 1976: north is at the top.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.08544" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="161.6158952" CenterY="-59.63822496" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Eta Carinae nebula, NGC 3372" OffsetX="0.19672781722399998" OffsetY="-0.09657842425999999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="10.264427539999998" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02175/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:64276267975014bc743f1642">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-02175/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02175/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-04745&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2003-10-06T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CYG" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="44.325010889135775" Magnitude="0" Name="Pelican Nebula Ionization Front" Opacity="100" RA="20.836293271859" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-04745/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.48724488">
    <Description>These images were obtained with the MOSAIC CCD camera at the prime focus of the Mayall 4-meter reflector, and show a 36 arc minute field of view that contains the well known Pelican nebula, located at the northwest rim of the giant HII region W80 (the North America/Pelican Nebula) in Cygnus. These narrow-band images, obtained in the red light of recombining hydrogen atoms and singly ionized sulfur, reveal a population of hitherto unrecognized shocks which trace outflows from forming stars embedded within the molecular clouds that rim the nebula. The visibility of these protostellar outflows is enhanced by the ultraviolet radiation field of several massive stars located off the image. This radiation is eroding the surrounding molecular cloud. In regions shadowed by dense clumps of gas and dust, parts of the dense and cold molecular cloud survive to produce the long pillars of dusty material. A faint jet (known as Herbig-Haro object 555) squirts out of the tip of one of the pillars, apparently indicating the presence of an unseen protostar. North is up and west is to the right in this image. This image was produced by NOAO Survey Program &amp;quot;Deep Imaging Survey of Nearby Star-Forming Clouds&amp;quot;, and is the subject of a related press release. Note: the large “donut” in the middle is an artifact of the coma corrector in the KPNO 4-meter that was not completely removed in the reduction. This is the full size image complete with ragged edges revealing the dither pattern of the different fields. A clipped version at lower resolution is also available.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.1763712" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="312.4039086" CenterY="44.06446529" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Pelican Nebula Ionization Front" OffsetX="-0.10129471587209998" OffsetY="0.26032807871004" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.17318727636648573" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-04745/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-04745" Xcxstatus="in:6427628a975014bc743f168d">
        <Credits>John Bally (U. Colorado), Bo Reipurth (U. Hawaii)/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-04745/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-04745/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
