  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;eso0809a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2008-04-15T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="HOR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-50.90389401746917" Magnitude="0" Name="The planet-host star Iota Horologii" Opacity="100" RA="2.698681482110662" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso0809a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.869234108064591">
    <Description>Using HARPS on the ESO 3.6-metre telescope at La Silla, astronomers were able to study in great detail the star Iota Horologii, known to harbour a giant planet, and make a very precise portrait of it: its temperature is 6150 K, its mass is 1.25 times that of the Sun, and its age is 625 million years. Moreover, the star is found to be more metal-rich than the Sun by about 50%. This means the star must have drifted from the Hyades cluster where it formed.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.8909783308833842" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="40.4925344715" CenterY="-50.9283241286" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The planet-host star Iota Horologii" OffsetX="0.008431778697060499" OffsetY="0.02420736464639953" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="1.5723288520760734" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso0809a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|eso0809a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>Digital Sky Survey/VirGO.</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0809a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso0809a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="6.90336" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m35&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:17+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="GEM" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="24.287430861387097" Magnitude="0" Name="M35, NGC 2158" Opacity="100" RA="6.141843076140759" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m35/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.90336">
    <Description>The open cluster M35 in the constellation Gemini contains a couple of hundred stars and is visible to the naked eye under good conditions. NGC2158, its fainter but compact neighbor to the south-west (lower right), is much harder to see in small telescopes, although prominent in this CCD image from a research telescope on a good mountain. M35 is about 2800 light-years away and some 110 million years old. This approximately true-color picture was created from twelve images taken in January 1997 using BVR colors, at the Burrell Schmidt telescope of Case Western Reserve University&amp;#x27;s Warner and Swasey Observatory located on Kitt Peak, near Tucson, Arizona. Image size</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.155072" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="92.40202491213766" CenterY="24.227385446133734" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M35, NGC 2158" OffsetX="0.24721972390320002" OffsetY="0.069444444954" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-2.261660519" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m35/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762af975014bc743f16dd">
        <Credits>N.A.Sharp/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m35/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m35/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="-8.383558439086874" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noirlab2012a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-07-01T13:26:19+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="IND" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-59.956269525149" Magnitude="0" Name="Striking image shows mass distribution of nearby galaxy cluster" Opacity="100" RA="22.0357901470836" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2012a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.96777006902621">
    <Description>Using the Dark Energy Camera at Cerro Tololo Inter-American Observatory, a Program of NSF’s NOIRLab, astronomers are measuring weak gravitational lensing to map the distribution of mass in nearby galaxy clusters. As well as producing remarkable images like this one, the information will shed light on the relationships between the constituent parts of nearby galaxy clusters, such as gas, stellar populations, and dark matter. The galaxy cluster Abell 3827 is the subject of this striking image. In addition to featuring a field of galaxies and foreground stars, the image is overlaid with a map with hues of red, blue and purple. Though they are attractive, these colors aren’t for decoration — they represent the distribution of mass in the heart of Abell 3827 with red showing the largest mass concentration.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="-1.19700410863616" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="330.26628749911794" CenterY="-60.03379625010785" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Striking image shows mass distribution of nearby galaxy cluster" OffsetX="0.11418806706888272" OffsetY="-0.072331468497724" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="180.21815838955914" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2012a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noirlab2012a" Xcxstatus="in:64276302975014bc743f178d">
        <Credits>CTIO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2012a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2012a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="-8.383558439086874" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noirlab2012c&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-07-01T13:45:46+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="IND" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-59.956269525149" Magnitude="0" Name="Main image without lensing map" Opacity="100" RA="22.0357901470836" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2012c/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.96777006902621">
    <Description>This image is a snapshot from the Local Volume Complete Cluster Survey (LoVoCCS), a survey program investigating 107 nearby galaxy clusters.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="-1.19700410863616" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="330.26628749911794" CenterY="-60.03379625010785" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Main image without lensing map" OffsetX="0.11418806706888272" OffsetY="-0.072331468497724" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="180.21815838955914" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2012c/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noirlab2012c" Xcxstatus="in:64276303975014bc743f178f">
        <Credits>NOIRLab</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2012c/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2012c/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="7" Classification="Galaxy" Constellation="AND" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="41.5927244444445" Magnitude="0" Name="PHAT M31" Opacity="100" RA="0.7436091666666655" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2020/07_phat_m31/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7">
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.8197882908523313" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="11.686498572218326" CenterY="41.90823522094287" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Sharpest ever view of the Andromeda Galaxy;Andromeda Galaxy;M 31;Messier 31" OffsetX="-0.483202756087923" OffsetY="-0.154475340368076" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-236.10668016489333" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="9" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2020/07_phat_m31/{1}/{3}/{3}_{2}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:649ba2b61e6961df10c19447">
        <Credits>NASA, ESA, J. Dalcanton (University of Washington, USA), B.
        F. Williams (University of Washington, USA), L. C. Johnson (University
        of Washington, USA), the PHAT team, and R. Gendler. Astrometric
        alignment by A. David Weigel (US Space &amp; Rocket Center).</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://esahubble.org/images/heic1502a/</CreditsUrl>
        <Description>This image, captured with the NASA/ESA Hubble Space
        Telescope, is the largest and sharpest image ever taken of the
        Andromeda galaxy — otherwise known as M31. The full image has 1.5
        billion pixels. You would need more than 600 HD television screens to
        display the whole image at once. It is the biggest Hubble image ever
        released and shows over 100 million stars and thousands of star
        clusters embedded in a section of the galaxy’s pancake-shaped disc
        stretching across over 40,000 light-years.</Description>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2020/07_phat_m31/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;m55-3point6-m_copy&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2009-12-03T23:19:36+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SGR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-30.933219404335293" Magnitude="0" Name="Messier 55 (M55) globular cluster" Opacity="100" RA="19.66568687479853" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/m55-3point6-m_copy/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.014852319103999">
    <Description>Globular cluster Messier 55 (M55, or NGC 6809) in the constellation Sagittarius, as imaged by the ESO 3.6-metre telescope on La Silla.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.99048685568" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="294.998738" CenterY="-30.964858" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Messier 55 (M55) globular cluster" OffsetX="0.01236026024677771" OffsetY="0.03132065563005908" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="1.52200209283" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/m55-3point6-m_copy/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|m55-3point6-m_copy" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
