  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noirlab2106d&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2021-01-14T09:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CET" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="2.158229157238543" Magnitude="0" Name="Wider view of irregular dwarf galaxy IC 1613 from the Dark Energy Survey" Opacity="100" RA="1.0962745563514729" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2106d/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.448579549818199">
    <Description>The irregular dwarf galaxy IC 1613 contains some 100 million stars and is a member of our Local Group of galaxies, which also includes our Milky Way, the Andromeda spiral galaxy, and the Magellanic Clouds.  This image is an excerpt from the Dark Energy Survey, which has released a massive, public collection of astronomical data and calibrated images from six years of work. The Dark Energy Survey is a global collaboration that includes the Department of Energy&amp;#x27;s (DOE) Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory (Fermilab), the National Center for Supercomputing Applications (NCSA), and NSF’s NOIRLab. The image was taken with the Dark Energy Camera, fabricated by DOE, on the Víctor M. Blanco 4-meter Telescope. The immensity and depth of the survey can be appreciated by diving into this zoomable image.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.1964463465119743" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="16.4441914224" CenterY="2.15830218253" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Wider view of irregular dwarf galaxy IC 1613 from the Dark Energy Survey" OffsetX="7.30252897041e-05" OffsetY="7.30252897041e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-90" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2106d/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noirlab2106d" Xcxstatus="in:64276313975014bc743f17b1">
        <Credits>DES/CTIO/NOIRLab/NSF/DOE/AURAAcknowledgments: Image processing: DES, Jen Miller (Gemini Observatory/NSF&amp;#x27;s NOIRLab), Travis Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage), Mahdi Zamani &amp;amp; Davide de Martin</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2106d/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2106d/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02637&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:14+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SCO" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-32.24065288376476" Magnitude="0" Name="M6, NGC 6405; Butterfly Cluster" Opacity="100" RA="17.670760408875605" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02637/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.47864">
    <Description>The open star cluster M6, or NGC6405, is also known as the Butterfly Cluster. M6 is visible to the naked eye in the constellation Scorpius. The visually most conspicuous star is rather reddish and is actually a slow, semiregular variable. M6 is variously estimated as between 50 and 100 million years old. The images were taken in June 1995 at the Burrell Schmidt telescope of Case Western Reserve University&amp;#x27;s Warner and Swasey Observatory, during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.155072" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="265.1407896" CenterY="-32.21213891" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M6, NGC 6405; Butterfly Cluster" OffsetX="0.06811540546200001" OffsetY="-0.02613497908559999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-2.036294484" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02637/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:64276283975014bc743f167e">
        <Credits>N.A.Sharp, Mark Hanna, REU program/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-02637/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02637/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="7.4851884" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0157a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:01+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="AUR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="34.30767959809152" Magnitude="0" Name="AE Aurigae" Opacity="100" RA="5.271719101242965" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0157a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.4851884">
    <Description>This spectacular image of the star AE Aurigae and its surrounding nebulosity was taken at the National Science Foundation&amp;#x27;s 0.9-meter telescope on Kitt Peak with the NOAO Mosaic CCD camera. Located in the constellation of Auriga, the Charioteer, AE Aurigae is the bright blue star at the center of the image. The incredible energy from this hot, massive star is energizing the surrounding gas, causing it to glow dramatically. As a result it is also known as &amp;quot;the flaming star&amp;quot;. This false-color image was created by combining emission-line images taken in Hydrogen-alpha (yellow), Oxygen [OIII] (violet) and Sulfur [SII] (blue).</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.966656" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="79.40386940470212" CenterY="34.32646955457235" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="AE Aurigae" OffsetX="0.015965848635999987" OffsetY="0.270697378204" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.50205055000015" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0157a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0157a" Xcxstatus="in:642762e8975014bc743f1754">
        <Credits>T.A.Rector and B.A.Wolpa/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0157a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0157a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
