  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02461&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:18+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="TAU" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="24.171466110064472" Magnitude="0" Name="M45, Pleiades" Opacity="100" RA="3.7697054725154673" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02461/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.6296">
    <Description>M45, a portion of the Pleiades open star cluster in the constellation Taurus, at a distance of some 410 light-years. The glow around the stars is interstellar dust which shines by reflected starlight. The entire group is contained within a span of 20 light-years, and is believed to be 20 million years old. KPNO 4-meter Mayall telescope, 1975.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.531904" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="56.44534588" CenterY="24.08573964" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M45, Pleiades" OffsetX="-0.09200901432000003" OffsetY="0.0851572022708" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.3780974048269558" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02461/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:64276281975014bc743f167a">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-02461/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02461/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;ann21023a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2021-06-08T10:17:36+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="HYA" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-28.393304953975466" Magnitude="0" Name="Dark Energy Camera's 1 millionth exposure" Opacity="100" RA="11.816945003762259" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/ann21023a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.646725776923775">
    <Description>The Dark Energy Camera&amp;#x27;s 1 millionth exposure. At the time of this exposure DECam was making an of a galaxy cluster. The 1 millionth exposure has been combined with 127 earlier exposures to make this view of the field.  This demonstrates the power of DECam and an archive of a million exposures. The biggest galaxy in the field is the face-on spiral galaxy ESO 440-11. Several galaxy clusters, thousands of background galaxies and a few handfuls of foreground Milky Way stars can be seen in this “random” image of the sky.  One of the most powerful digital cameras in the world, the Dark Energy Camera was designed specifically for the Dark Energy Survey (DES). It was funded by the Department of Energy (DOE) and was built and tested at DOE&amp;#x27;s Fermilab.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.2288885141504" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="177.471384786" CenterY="-28.2238018207" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Dark Energy Camera's 1 millionth exposure" OffsetX="0.1907260987956176" OffsetY="-0.17007552836661852" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.11996231169115491" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/ann21023a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|ann21023a" Xcxstatus="in:6427623b975014bc743f15e5">
        <Credits>Dark Energy Survey/DOE/FNAL/DECam/CTIO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA Acknowledgments: T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage/NSF’s NOIRLab), M. Zamani (NSF’s NOIRLab) &amp;amp; D. de Martin (NSF’s NOIRLab)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/ann21023a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/ann21023a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;eso2309a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2023-06-27T14:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="MON" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="0.21365458318494776" Magnitude="0" Name="The Sh2-284 nebula, imaged by the VLT Survey Telescope" Opacity="100" RA="6.75249693896449" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso2309a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.649700606479758">
    <Description>This spectacular picture of the Sh2-284 nebula has been captured in great detail by the VLT Survey Telescope at ESO’s Paranal Observatory. Sh2-284 is a star formation region, and at its centre there is a cluster of young stars, dubbed Dolidze 25. The radiation from this cluster is powerful enough to ionise the hydrogen gas in the nebula’s cloud. It is this ionisation that produces its bright orange and red colours. This image is part of the VST Photometric Hα Survey of the Southern Galactic Plane and Bulge, led by Janet Drew at the University of Hertfordshire in the UK.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.9738859548085944" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="101.287454123" CenterY="0.213595141916" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Sh2-284 nebula, imaged by the VLT Survey Telescope" OffsetX="2.972209586913853e-05" OffsetY="2.97206407107115e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.037141578119985" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso2309a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|eso2309a" Xcxstatus="in:6552f2c55dff7c3291e3b0ea">
        <Credits>ESO/VPHAS+ team. Acknowledgement: CASU</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso2309a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso2309a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
