  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="7.907603823745559" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m39&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2004-06-29T19:03:39+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CYG" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="48.464070800144114" Magnitude="0" Name="M39, NGC 7092" Opacity="100" RA="21.53028081313586" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m39/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.907603823745559">
    <Description>M39 is a loose, widely spaced open cluster in the constellation Cygnus. Quite close to Earth at about 800 light-years away, M39 has around 30 members and is two to three hundred million years old. This picture is a combination of 33 dithered frames in BVR colors taken with the Mosaic camera at the WIYN 0.9-meter telescope on Kitt Peak, near Tucson, Arizona, on the night of September 15th, 2003. This camera uses eight separate CCD detectors to cover a large area of the sky, but requires small dither motions between multiple exposures to fill in the gaps between the individual chips.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.9622562466447359" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="322.9544682967236" CenterY="48.4637358760129" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M39, NGC 7092" OffsetX="0.00011746292073299998" OffsetY="0.00011746292073299998" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.7799999999999998" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m39/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m39" Xcxstatus="in:642762b1975014bc743f16e0">
        <Credits>Heidi Schweiker/WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m39/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m39/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;eso1723a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2017-07-27T12:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="ORI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-5.4786707566698025" Magnitude="0" Name="The Orion Nebula and cluster from the VLT Survey Telescope" Opacity="100" RA="5.58562486260458" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso1723a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.91538558418722">
    <Description>OmegaCAM — the wide-field optical camera on ESO’s VLT Survey Telescope (VST) — has captured the spectacular Orion Nebula and its associated cluster of young stars in great detail,  producing this beautiful new image. This famous object, the birthplace of many massive stars, is one of the closest stellar nurseries, at a distance of about 1350 light-years.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.9400750895169536" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="83.7844027714" CenterY="-5.47870026672" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Orion Nebula and cluster from the VLT Survey Telescope" OffsetX="2.960319655635e-05" OffsetY="2.960319655635e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.17999999999999985" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="7" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso1723a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|eso1723a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>ESO/G. Beccari</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1723a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso1723a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02274&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:49+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="TRI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="30.653868239510402" Magnitude="0" Name="M33, NGC 598" Opacity="100" RA="1.5637106717221305" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02274/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="7.9248358095165">
    <Description>The Triangulum Galaxy, M33, a type Sc spiral, was imaged by the KPNO 4-meter Mayall telescope in 1975. A member of our Local Group of galaxies, it is just visible with the naked eye.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.272946175520768" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="23.4560195652" CenterY="30.6535559471" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M33, NGC 598" OffsetX="0.000310777874883" OffsetY="0.000310777874883" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.2799999999999998" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02274/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-02274" Xcxstatus="in:64276273975014bc743f165d">
