  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02190&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:11+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="MON" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="4.959219090029506" Magnitude="0" Name="Rosette Nebula, NGC 2237" Opacity="100" RA="6.541310619944884" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02190/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="21.909599999999998">
    <Description>The Rosette Nebula, NGC2237, in the constellation of Monoceros, as seen by the Curtis Schmidt telescope at the Cerro Tololo Interamerican Observatory. The nebula is about 2600 light-years away and about 55 light-years across. The central star cluster is known as NGC2244. North is at the top.</Description>
