  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;eso2320a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2023-12-21T14:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CEN" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-63.175138757239694" Magnitude="0" Name="The Running Chicken Nebula" Opacity="100" RA="11.58666855727729" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso2320a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="22.43784561390995">
    <Description>The Running Chicken Nebula comprises several clouds, all of which we can see in this vast image from the VLT Survey Telescope (VST), hosted at ESO’s Paranal site. This 1.5-billion pixel image spans an area in the sky of about 25 full Moons. The clouds shown in wispy pink plumes are full of gas and dust, illuminated by the young and hot stars within them.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="3.895308237183386" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="173.800094229" CenterY="-63.1751684702" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Running Chicken Nebula" OffsetX="5.94376867246e-05" OffsetY="2.97188433623e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.011370379878429001" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="8" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso2320a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|eso2320a" Xcxstatus="in:65860314293f0a574636f246">
        <Credits>ESO/VPHAS+ team. Acknowledgement: CASU</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso2320a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso2320a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0136a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:02+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-59.612084730559346" Magnitude="0" Name="Carina Nebula (NGC 3372)" Opacity="100" RA="10.734398558936656" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0136a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="22.504708799999996">
    <Description>This image shows a giant star-forming region in the southern sky known as the Carina Nebula (NGC3372), combining the light from 3 different filters tracing emission from oxygen (blue), hydrogen (green), and sulfur (red). The color is also representative of the temperature in the ionized gas: blue is relatively hot and red is cooler. The Carina Nebula is a good example of how very massive stars rip apart the molecular clouds that give birth to them. The bright star near the center of the image is Eta Carinae, which is one of the most massive and luminous stars known. This picture is a composite of several exposures made with the Curtis Schmidt telescope at the Cerro Tololo Interamerican Observatory. We also have a broad-band optical image, approximately true color, made photographically with the Curtis Schmidt.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="2.6378239999999997" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="161.332597" CenterY="-60.47667269" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Carina Nebula (NGC 3372)" OffsetX="0.14497761095159997" OffsetY="0.8669514714279999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-1.0062460440000003" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0136a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0136a" Xcxstatus="in:642762e6975014bc743f1751">
        <Credits>Nathan Smith, University of Minnesota/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0136a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0136a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
