  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="VEL" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-43.929276094714766" Magnitude="0" Name="Ghostly Stellar Tendrils of the Vela Supernova Remnant" Opacity="100" RA="8.521966591526821" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2406a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="27.460579611544446">
    <Description>This colorful web of wispy gas filaments is the Vela Supernova Remnant, imaged by the Department of Energy-fabricated Dark Energy Camera (DECam). The Vela Supernova Remnant is merely the ghost of a massive star that once was. When the star exploded 11,000 years ago, its outer layers were violently stripped away and flung into the surrounding region, driving the shockwave that is still visible today. As the shockwave expands into the surrounding region, the hot, energized gas flies away from the point of detonation, compressing and interacting with the interstellar medium to produce the stringy blue and yellow filaments seen in the image. The Vela Supernova Remnant is a gigantic structure, spanning almost 100 light-years and extending to twenty times the diameter of the full Moon in the night sky.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="4.917279792030894" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="127.82955105487869" CenterY="-43.92931354505953" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Ghostly Stellar Tendrils of the Vela Supernova Remnant" OffsetX="3.751586755394664e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.1" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="8" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2406a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcorepipe_ids="noirlab|noirlab2406a" Xcxstatus="in:6711e2ac601b9591d930c913">
        <Credits>CTIO/NOIRLab/DOE/NSF/AURA Image Processing: T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage/NSF’s NOIRLab), M. Zamani &amp; D. de Martin (NSF’s NOIRLab)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2406a/</CreditsUrl>
        <Description>This colorful web of wispy gas filaments is the Vela Supernova Remnant, imaged by the Department of Energy-fabricated Dark Energy Camera (DECam). The Vela Supernova Remnant is merely the ghost of a massive star that once was. When the star exploded 11,000 years ago, its outer layers were violently stripped away and flung into the surrounding region, driving the shockwave that is still visible today. As the shockwave expands into the surrounding region, the hot, energized gas flies away from the point of detonation, compressing and interacting with the interstellar medium to produce the stringy blue and yellow filaments seen in the image. The Vela Supernova Remnant is a gigantic structure, spanning almost 100 light-years and extending to twenty times the diameter of the full Moon in the night sky.</Description>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2406a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
