  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="80.529" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0601b&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2006-01-09T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="DOR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-68.3594350603908" Magnitude="0" Name="Large Magellanic Cloud" Opacity="100" RA="5.29341940293126" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0601b/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="80.529">
    <Description>The glowing gas of the interstellar medium (ISM) is the breeding ground for the formation of new stars, and the cemetery where the ashes of dead stars ultimately return. A team led by astronomers from the National Optical Astronomy Observatory (NOAO) has conducted a new study called the Magellanic Cloud Emission Line Survey (MCELS) that focused expressly on the ISM in the Large Magellanic Cloud and Small Magellanic Cloud—the nearest major galaxies to the Milky Way. More than 1,500 individual images from MCELS have been assembled into this large mosaic of the Large Magellanic Cloud, released to the public in Washington, DC, in January 2006 at the annual meeting of the American Astronomical Society. See also NOAO Press Release 06-01.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="10.24" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="77.81610770323486" CenterY="-67.95358039168705" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Large Magellanic Cloud" OffsetX="-0.6246554575" OffsetY="-0.34706212250000024" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="1.4567576888000007" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0601b/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0601b" Xcxstatus="in:642762ef975014bc743f1763">
        <Credits>C. Smith, S. Points, the MCELS Team and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0601b/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0601b/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;eso1914a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2019-09-13T09:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="MEN" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-70.0727064369465" Magnitude="0" Name="The Large Magellanic Cloud revealed by VISTA" Opacity="100" RA="5.419556622906693" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso1914a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="87.699547963247">
    <Description>ESO’s VISTA telescope reveals a remarkable image of the Large Magellanic Cloud, one of our nearest galactic neighbours. VISTA has been surveying this galaxy and its sibling the Small Magellanic Cloud, as well as their surroundings, in unprecedented detail. This survey allows astronomers to observe a large number of stars, opening up new opportunities to study stellar evolution, galactic dynamics, and variable stars.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="8.80847574892544" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="81.2941037588" CenterY="-70.0729864524" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Large Magellanic Cloud revealed by VISTA" OffsetX="0.00026881334683" OffsetY="0.00026881334683" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="2.439999999999996" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="7" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso1914a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|eso1914a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>ESO/VMC Survey</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1914a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/eso1914a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noirlab2030a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-12-01T08:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="MEN" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-71.56477446533543" Magnitude="0" Name="Deepest, widest view of the Large Magellanic Cloud from SMASH" Opacity="100" RA="5.252857508824116" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2030a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="92.52385743560467">
    <Description>Part of the SMASH dataset showing an unprecedented wide-angle view of the Large Magellanic Cloud. The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are the largest satellite galaxies of the Milky Way and, unlike the rest of the satellite galaxies, are still actively forming stars — and at a rapid pace. The depth of these survey data can be appreciated by the number of stars visible in the outer regions of the galaxy, as seen in the lower part of the image.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="11.369240647761918" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="78.7957840796" CenterY="-71.566506171" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Deepest, widest view of the Large Magellanic Cloud from SMASH" OffsetX="0.0013878467587599998" OffsetY="0.0013878467587599998" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="16.920000000000027" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2030a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noirlab2030a" Xcxstatus="in:6427630e975014bc743f17a5">
        <Credits>CTIO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/SMASH/D. Nidever (Montana State University) Acknowledgment: Image processing: Travis Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage), Mahdi Zamani &amp;amp; Davide de Martin</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2030a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2030a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
